  "chapter": 19,
  "title": "Counting On --- Six to Ten, and What They Prove",
  "drivablePrefix": 6,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:c4e324dcb73d0c60",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-six",
      "sequence": 900,
      "title": "षट् (ṣaṭ) — six",
      "headword": "षट्",
      "romanization": "ṣaṭ",
      "gloss": "six",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:7796c2d4775216aa",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can count to five. The next five are where the family resemblance becomes impossible to miss."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: षट्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "षट् (ṣaṭ) — \"six.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say it beside its relatives: Latin sex, Greek hex, English six. The Sanskrit form looks least like the others on the page, because ṣ is a retroflex sound English has no letter for — but it is the same word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This chapter is the clearest evidence a beginner can be shown that Sanskrit, Latin and English are relatives rather than neighbours. The numbers are the proof, because numbers are borrowed less often than almost anything else."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One number, and a claim you are about to see supported four more times."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ṣaṭ — \"six\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *ṣaṭ* — \"six\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "its Latin and English cousins — sex, six",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: its Latin and English cousins — *sex*, *six*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "one to six, in order",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: one to six, in order]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What are the Latin and English cousins of षट् (ṣaṭ)? (Sex and six.) Why are numbers good evidence of relatedness? (They are rarely borrowed.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-seven",
      "sequence": 910,
      "title": "सप्त (sapta) — seven",
      "headword": "सप्त",
      "romanization": "sapta",
      "gloss": "seven",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:5a6a77ac75c4b4da",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The second of the five, and the resemblance is now visible without squinting."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सप्त",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सप्त (sapta) — \"seven.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin septem. Greek hepta. English seven. Line them up and the shape is one shape: s-p-t under the vowels, with Greek turning the initial s into a rough breathing and English softening the -pt- to -v-."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The Sanskrit form is not the ancestor of the others — that is a common misunderstanding worth heading off. All four descend from a shared parent nobody wrote down. Sanskrit is simply an old, well-recorded cousin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two numbers, and one correction that matters: Sanskrit is a cousin of Latin and English, not their ancestor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "sapta — \"seven\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *sapta* — \"seven\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "septem, hepta, seven after it",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *septem*, *hepta*, *seven* after it]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "whether Sanskrit is the parent of English (it is not — a cousin)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: whether Sanskrit is the parent of English (it is not — a cousin)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which consonants survive across all four forms? (S, p, t.) Is Sanskrit the ancestor of Latin and English? (No — a cousin; the parent was never written down.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-eight",
      "sequence": 920,
      "title": "अष्ट (aṣṭa) — eight",
      "headword": "अष्ट",
      "romanization": "aṣṭa",
      "gloss": "eight",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:0f20bbacc158a079",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Eight — and a word that still shows its seams."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: अष्ट",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "अष्ट (aṣṭa) — \"eight.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Latin octo, Greek okto, English eight (whose silent -gh- is the ghost of a consonant that used to be pronounced)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "There is an old and appealing observation about this one: the form behaves like a dual — the grammatical number Sanskrit uses for things that come in pairs. Eight would then be \"two fours,\" counted on two hands with the thumbs left out. It is a hypothesis rather than a settled fact, and it is offered here as one, because a good story is not the same as evidence."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three numbers, and a reminder about how to hold an etymology that is attractive but unproven: enjoy it, label it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "aṣṭa — \"eight\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *aṣṭa* — \"eight\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "octo, eight",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *octo*, *eight*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "whether the \"two fours\" story is fact or hypothesis",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: whether the \"two fours\" story is fact or hypothesis]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which English word is अष्ट (aṣṭa) related to, and what is its silent -gh-? (Eight — the ghost of a pronounced consonant.) Is the \"two fours\" reading established? (No — a hypothesis.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-nine",
      "sequence": 930,
      "title": "नवन् (navan) — nine",
      "headword": "नवन्",
      "romanization": "navan",
      "gloss": "nine",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:fa7aab7df2211af7",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nine — and a word you have met before meaning something else entirely."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: नवन्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नवन् (navan) — \"nine.\" Latin novem, English nine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the catch, and it is worth meeting deliberately rather than tripping over. You already learned नव (nava) meaning \"new.\" They look nearly identical and they are not the same word:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नव (nava) — new. Latin novus, English new. नवन् (navan) — nine. Latin novem, English nine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two distinct ancient words that drifted into near-identical shapes. Latin kept them apart (novus against novem) and so did English (new against nine). Sanskrit did not, quite — and a reader who assumes one word here will misread sentences."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A pair of look-alikes, separated on purpose. This is the most common way to get a word wrong: assuming that similar shape means shared origin."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "navan — \"nine\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *navan* — \"nine\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nava — \"new\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nava* — \"new\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "which Latin word goes with each (novem, novus)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: which Latin word goes with each (*novem*, *novus*)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What is the difference between नव and नवन् (navan)? (New, and nine.) Are they the same word? (No — two ancient words that drifted into similar shapes.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-ten",
      "sequence": 940,
      "title": "दश (daśa) — ten",
      "headword": "दश",
      "romanization": "daśa",
      "gloss": "ten",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:82713d2b3e2cc568",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The last of them, and the one hiding inside words you use every week."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: दश",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "दश (daśa) — \"ten.\" Latin decem, Greek deka, English ten."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The Latin form is the one that pays. Decem gives English decimal, decade, December — which was the tenth month before the calendar was rearranged and left the name stranded two months out of place."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So दश (daśa), decem and ten are one word, and a fossil of the old counting survives in the name of the wrong month."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can count to ten in Sanskrit, and every one of the second five arrived attached to a word you already knew."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "daśa — \"ten\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *daśa* — \"ten\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "one to ten, straight through",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: one to ten, straight through]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "why December is misnamed",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: why December is misnamed]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which English words carry Latin decem? (Decimal, decade, December.) Why is December's name wrong? (It was the tenth month before the calendar changed.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C19-number-family",
      "sequence": 950,
      "title": "सङ्ख्याशब्दाः (saṅkhyā-śabdāḥ) — the numbers, and what they prove",
      "headword": "सङ्ख्याशब्दाः",
      "romanization": "saṅkhyā-śabdāḥ",
      "gloss": "the numbers, and what they prove",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:10a794f5dd5d1ae0",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ten numbers is enough to see something no single word could have shown you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सङ्ख्याशब्दाः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Set the ten side by side with Latin and English and the correspondence is not occasional — it is regular:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "एक one, द्व two, त्रि three, चतुर् four, पञ्च five, षट् (ṣaṭ) six, सप्त (sapta) seven, अष्ट (aṣṭa) eight, नवन् (navan) nine, दश (daśa) ten."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Where Sanskrit has p, Latin often has p and English often has f. Where Sanskrit has d, English often has t. Those are not impressions; they are regular correspondences, and their regularity is what turned the study of language into something testable in the nineteenth century."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is what the numbers prove. Not that these languages borrowed from each other, but that they inherited from something older — and that the inheritance followed rules consistent enough to predict."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You can count to ten, and you have seen the single piece of evidence that founded historical linguistics."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all ten, in order",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all ten, in order]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English cousin of each",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English cousin of each]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what the regularity proves — inheritance, not borrowing",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what the regularity proves — inheritance, not borrowing]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What makes the correspondences evidence rather than coincidence? (They are regular.) What do they show? (Common inheritance from an older language.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
